        "department": null,
        "description": "Isn't talking solely about gender/class/caste enough? Why intersectionality and not double discrimination? Are identities becoming too niche with more specific positionalities being outlined (Cissexual, Heterosexual Savarna, Middle-Class Man)? How far can we go and where does this take us? This course will equip students with an understanding of the historical and theoretical foundations; contemporary debates; and relevant themes of intersectionality. It seeks to familiarise students with the core theoretical and methodological concerns of doing intersectional research. With this main objective in mind, the course is broadly divided in two parts: the first part engages with the historical background, concepts, theory, and approach envisioned by intersectionality. The second part, introduces ways of conceptualising and understanding intersectionality and related issues, in various spheres. By the end of the course, the students will be able to develop a sound understanding of intersectionality and its application.",
